WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.023
You're studying comedy,

00:00:01.023 --> 00:00:02.660
do you have any questions for me?

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:04.500
Is there anything I can do to help you?

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.300
Okay, so the biggest question I have,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.900
I remember I was listening
to your podcast once,

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.930
because literally for Spotify,

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.050
you guys were like my #1 podcast

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.700
I think 10,000 minutes of
me listening to you guys.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:17.533
Yeah.

00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:19.490
Oh, that's so sweet, that's so sweet.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:20.323
Thank you.

00:00:20.323 --> 00:00:21.420
Yeah, so a question I had

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.560
you said that when you
first started doing improv

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:24.720
you would get really nervous

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.640
and I'm pretty sure a teacher
of yours had to pull you aside

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.190
and be like, "Calm down."

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.020
And I was wondering how
you got through that

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.640
and how you became who you are now.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:34.723
I think a lot of people

00:00:34.723 --> 00:00:39.723
and myself included think
your job is to become funny

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.840
and to become this person who's known

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.840
or a celebrity or whatever
you want to call it.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.600
And I think it's not that.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.670
I think if you're doing it the right way,

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.600
you're figuring out a way

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.180
to become the person you already were

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.950
in an unusual situation.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.220
So when you say at home

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.710
and with your friends
you're Richard Pryor,

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.760
and then you get up on stage.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.330
Yes, that's the trick, is figuring out

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:09.210
how can I bring that
person who's funny at home?

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.070
How can I bring that person up on stage?

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:17.060
And that takes a little
doing and takes time.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.340
But the good news is you have that person,

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.780
that person's fully formed.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.890
That person is the person you are

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.680
with your friends and family.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.330
What's tricky is when people say,

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.550
I'm not funny with my friends and family,

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.520
but I want to be a comedian.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.423
You're like, well,

00:01:35.203 --> 00:01:36.120
(laughs)

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.490
I don't think that's going to work

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.712
but I do think that
that's just called time.

00:01:40.712 --> 00:01:44.395
If you spend enough
time in front of people,

00:01:44.395 --> 00:01:46.133
I mean you can watch the first,

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.592
you know year of me on
late night television.

00:01:49.592 --> 00:01:52.290
And I look like a guy who's trying

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.860
to do a good job as a comedy host.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.850
What happens is through the sheer

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.120
just amount of shows we had to do

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.800
and the sheer stress and whatever it was,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:03.860
that all fell away,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.670
and soon I just felt
like I was always on TV

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.005
so the real me came out.

00:02:08.005 --> 00:02:09.820
The real me ended up coming out.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:10.950
And so that's what you do

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:15.210
is you get your stage time
until the real person comes out.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.049
That's such good advice

00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:16.882
Does that make sense?

00:02:16.882 --> 00:02:17.715
No, it makes perfect sense.

00:02:17.715 --> 00:02:19.180
Yeah, is it okay if I charge you for it?

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.013
No.

00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:22.090
Well, since like I'm a student,

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:24.920
so I don't have much money in general.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.030
I'd prefer if you didn't.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.490
Well, what I like to do is
it'll be like a student loan

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.348
and it will be a very high rate.

00:02:30.348 --> 00:02:31.181
What

00:02:31.181 --> 00:02:32.014
Not a good rate.

00:02:32.014 --> 00:02:33.760
What happens if she doesn't pay?

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.494
You already gave her the advice.

00:02:35.494 --> 00:02:36.327
(laughs)

00:02:36.327 --> 00:02:40.468
Yeah, well I will repossess
your comedy career whatever

00:02:40.468 --> 00:02:41.301
Oh okay

00:02:41.301 --> 00:02:42.220
career in comedy,

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.080
I will come and I will repossess it,

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.130
(laughing)

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.690
or I'll say I now have
80% of it, you know,

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:49.523
it's a really,

00:02:49.523 --> 00:02:51.420
look you never should
have gotten on this call.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.970
This was a big mistake.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.540
'Cause now, Chelby, it's just like,

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.010
I just have a huge controlling
interest in your career,

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.090
and there's no way out.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.390
There's really no way out.

